</ul><h1>A Gimmick?</h1><p>Okay, so here’s the official Urbanista ad for these earbuds:</p>
<p><iframe allowfullscreen class="youtube" frameborder="0" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/nR-M6XhUeFA?autohide=1&color=white&showinfo=0&theme=light"></iframe></p>
<p>You don’t have to watch it, though, because it basically takes a minute and a half to tell you the same thing that the product title on this page does. And no, that isn’t <em>that</em> long, but it’s a bit much for an earbud promo video.</p>
<p>What’s more interesting than its content, though, is the subtext the ad accidentally communicates.</p>
<p>The first thing Urbanista highlights is the self-charging case. The second thing is the material the case is made of that allows it to be self-charging (something called ‘Powerfoyle’ that can apparently recharge in natural and artificial light).</p>
<p>Thirty seconds into the video (in other words, a third of the way through), we get our first mention of anything related to sound. And even this is just to mention their active noise-cancelling function. Then there’s a bit about transparency mode, the ability to connect to multiple devices at once, color options, and the app.</p>
<p>Somehow, they take an entire minute and a half to explain these earbuds, and at no point do they say a single thing about sound quality, which inadvertently tells the viewer: these are a gimmick product and not to be taken seriously for their intended purpose of listening to music or podcasts or whatever.</p>
<p>What’s funny is that this is wrong. Or partially wrong, at least.</p>
<p>And we know from a reliable source: you.</p>
<p><a href="https://meh.com/forum/topics/urbanista-phoenix-hybrid-active-noise-cancelling-earbuds-with-solar-charging-1">Last time we sold these things</a>, the comments made two things clear:</p>
<ol>
<li>
<p>The solar-charging function is a slow, if not outright useless gimmick; and…</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>The overall sound quality is better than expected.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>And yes, point 1 might be a dealbreaker if you were to pay the ridiculous price of $258 they once went for on Amazon. But for $25? They can just be buds that under-deliver on their charging potential and over-deliver on sound quality.</p>
<p>And let’s be honest, it’s better than the other way around, right?</p>